# MASTER LINUX — 18 Essential Linux Commands

----------------------------------------------------------
| No. | Command | Purpose                                |
| --- | ------- | -------------------------------------- |
| 01  | `ls`    | List files and directories             |
| 02  | `cd`    | Change directories                     |
| 03  | `pwd`   | Print the current working directory    |
| 04  | `mkdir` | Create directories                     |
| 05  | `touch` | Create files or update timestamps      |
| 06  | `cp`    | Copy files and directories             |
| 07  | `mv`    | Move or rename files and directories   |
| 08  | `rm`    | Remove files and directories           |
| 09  | `cat`   | Display file contents                  |
| 10  | `less`  | Read files page by page                |
| 11  | `grep`  | Search text using patterns             |
| 12  | `find`  | Find files and directories             |
| 13  | `chmod` | Change file and directory permissions  |
| 14  | `chown` | Change file and directory ownership    |
| 15  | `ps`    | Display process information            |
| 16  | `top`   | Monitor running processes in real time |
| 17  | `kill`  | Send signals to processes              |
| 18  | `df`    | Check filesystem disk space            |
----------------------------------------------------------


## Quick command examples ##

    
    01. ls -la
    02. cd /home/user
    03. pwd
    04. mkdir projects
    05. touch file.txt
    06. cp file.txt backup.txt
    07. mv old.txt new.txt
    08. rm file.txt
    09. cat file.txt
    10. less file.txt
    11. grep "error" app.log
    12. find . -name "*.txt"
    13. chmod 755 script.sh
    14. sudo chown user:user file.txt
    15. ps aux
    16. top
    17. kill <PID>
    18. df -h


## Important notes ##

- `ls -la` shows hidden files and detailed file information.
- `cp -r` copies directories recursively.
- `rm -r` removes directories and their contents. Use it carefully.
- `grep -i` searches without case sensitivity.
- `chmod 755` grants the owner read/write/execute permissions and others read/execute permissions.
- `df -h` displays disk usage in human-readable units.
- `kill <PID>` sends a termination signal by default; use `kill -9 <PID>` only when necessary.

Recommended learning order: Start with navigation (`ls`, `cd`, `pwd`), learn file operations (`mkdir`, `touch`, `cp`, `mv`, `rm`), then practice searching, permissions, and process management.
